  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Is Your Entrance Truly Accessible?",
    "text": "Is Your Entrance Truly Accessible?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "It's easy to assume an entrance is accessible, but sometimes those small details make a big difference. True accessibility means a smooth, safe, and easy passage for everyone. A big question is often how to deal with that front step barrier – do you need a ramp, or maybe just a threshold modification? You also have to consider if changes will allow your door to operate normally; you don't want to fix one problem and create another. We often see folks focus just on the step itself and miss the landing area, or they install a ramp that blocks the door swing entirely. Another common issue is under-sizing the platform or clearance needed. For genuine accessibility, you really need to assess the entire entrance, from the approach right through to the door's full swing. Remember, things like the ramp slope, width, and threshold height have specific standards. If you're wondering if your home really meets those accessibility needs, especially for permanent changes or structural elements, it's wise to get an expert to check it out. They can make sure everything is up to par.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
